      "headline": "Why Normal People Do Crazy Things",
      "keyInsight": "When someone cuts you off in traffic, you assume they are a jerk. When you cut someone off, you blame your blind spot or an emergency. This double standard blinds us every single day. We love sorting the world into neat boxes of good apples and bad apples. But put a sweet, crisp apple into hot spiced vinegar, and it turns into a sour pickle every single time. It is not defective fruit. It is simply swimming in vinegar. The most shocking truth of human life is that ordinary, decent people can act terribly under the wrong pressure. Bad behavior rarely comes from evil villains twirling their mustaches in dark alleys. Most often, normal folks find themselves caught in a tricky room with hidden pressures. If you only blame someone's personality, you miss the invisible currents pushing their boat.",
      "content": "Imagine watching a chef drop a pot of soup on the kitchen floor. You might laugh and think the chef is clumsy. But look closer at the kitchen floor. Someone spilled cooking oil on the tiles right by the stove. The chef was not clumsy at all. The floor was an ice rink. We constantly judge other drivers, coworkers, and neighbors while ignoring the slippery oil on their floor.\n\nSocial psychology is the study of how people influence other people. It looks at the invisible invisible magnetic pull between humans. We like to imagine ourselves as lone wolves steering our own ships. In reality, we adjust our sails the second another ship enters the harbor. We talk quieter in fancy restaurants. We walk faster in crowded train stations. Other people constantly reshape our everyday choices without saying a single word.\n\nThis leads to Aronson's first great law: people who do crazy things are not necessarily crazy. When good people make terrible blunders, the environment is usually doing the heavy lifting. If a factory line moves too fast, workers skip safety steps. They are not careless by nature. The speed of the belt forces their hand.\n\nOnce you grasp this idea, your view of the world flips upside down. Instead of asking what is wrong with someone, you start asking about the room they are sitting in. Change the temperature of the room, and people blossom. Keep the room icy, and even the warmest soul will shiver.",
