      "headline": "The Pressure Cooker of Human Temper",
      "keyInsight": "Popular folk wisdom says that blowing off steam keeps anger from boiling over. Go punch a punching bag, yell into a pillow, or vent your rage, right? Science reveals the shocking truth: venting does not extinguish the fire, it pours lighter fluid straight onto the coals. Practicing anger just teaches your brain how to stay angry longer. The cooler path is discovering what turned up the heat in the first place.",
      "content": "Picture a heavy kitchen pressure cooker sitting on a hot gas stove. When frustration builds up inside a room, people assume anger is an invisible gas waiting to explode. But human temper is far more like a campfire than a pressure cooker. If you blow on the embers or feed them dry branches, the flames leap higher. Snapping at a coworker or aggressively honking in gridlock traffic does not drain the tank; it simply warms up the engine for the next round of hostility.\n\nAggression rarely appears out of thin air. It almost always begins with a blocked goal. Imagine waiting in a long line at the bakery on a rainy morning, your mouth watering for the last loaf of bread. If someone cuts right in front of you, the sudden roadblock triggers a wave of irritation. Add a sweltering room, sharp physical discomfort, or an accidental elbow nudge, and that irritation quickly catches fire. The environment acts as an amplifier, turning small snags into loud fireworks.\n\nFortunately, calm spreads just as quickly as conflict when given the chance. When people learn to spot the spark early\u2014like recognizing that a rude remark came from pure exhaustion rather than malice\u2014the urge to fight back evaporates. Building shared projects where everyone must row the same boat across rough water melts away hostility faster than any argument. Cooperation cools the room, while retaliation only keeps the furnace roaring.",
