      "id": "the-social-animal-ch-7",
      "order": 7,
      "chapterNumber": 7,
      "headline": "Why We Hate the Other Team Before the Match Even Starts",
      "keyInsight": "We love to believe that prejudice belongs only to nasty or broken minds. The unsettling truth is much simpler and far more widespread. Our brains run on high-speed mental shortcuts to conserve fuel. Like an overzealous sorting machine at an airport cargo bay, the mind tosses people into neat bins: 'My Crew' or 'The Outsiders.' Once someone lands in the outsider bin, your brain treats them like a cheap mass-produced clone, assuming they all look, talk, and think alike. Meanwhile, everyone in your own crew gets praised as a unique, complex diamond. When good fortune strikes someone in your circle, you say they worked hard for it. But when an outsider succeeds, you write it off as pure dumb luck. Worst of all, simply shoving rival groups into the same room backfires and turns up the heat. Real harmony only blooms when people must lock arms to push a stalled car out of the mud together.",
      "content": "Picture your brain as an overworked airport baggage sorter handling thousands of suitcases every minute. To keep from crashing, the sorter slaps hasty labels on every bag based purely on outer color. It assumes every green bag carries cheap plastic trinkets and every blue bag holds delicate Swiss watches. In human life, this instant sorting reflex is where prejudice starts. We divide our social world into two distinct piles: our familiar neighborhood team and everyone on the outside.\n\nOnce people land in that outsider bin, a strange distortion takes over our judgment. If a colleague on our team delivers a clumsy presentation, we blame bad sleep or a faulty projector. If an outsider delivers the exact same speech, we instantly claim they lack basic competence. We rewrite reality on the fly so our favored side always looks brilliant. We also invent scapegoats whenever life turns sour. When a sudden storm ruins the community garden, frustrated neighbors rarely blame shifting wind patterns. Instead, they point fingers at the new family down the road.\n\nSo how do we clear out this mental fog? Lecturing people on fairness usually changes nothing. Throwing rival groups together into a shared lunch hall can actually trigger worse food fights. The genuine cure resembles a stuck road trip. When a minibus breaks down on a muddy trail, rival passengers must push side-by-side to get rolling. Facing a shared challenge turns hostile strangers into working partners.",
